{"title": "Foundations of System Abstraction", "learningObjectives": ["Define the core abstraction layers that separate user applications from hardware execution.", "Identify the fundamental components of a Unix-like kernel that manage system resources.", "Recognize how abstraction enables modular design and security isolation in operating systems."], "beats": [
     {
       "title": "Process Definition and State",
       "claim": "A process combines an address space with execution state and operating-system resources.",
       "example": "A web server process maintains its own memory space, open file descriptors, and CPU scheduling context.",
       "visualDirection": "Display a diagram showing a process boundary with labeled address space, execution state, and OS resource handles.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "System Call Mechanism",
       "claim": "A system call transfers control from user code to a privileged kernel service.",
       "example": "A process invokes a read system call to request data from a file, transitioning to kernel mode.",
       "visualDirection": "Show a flowchart of control transfer between user space and kernel space with a labeled system call instruction.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Trap Handling and Context Switching",
       "claim": "A trap records processor state so the kernel can handle a system call, exception, or interrupt.",
       "example": "When a process triggers a page fault, the CPU traps the fault, saves registers, and transfers to the kernel handler.",
       "visualDirection": "Illustrate a trap sequence with a processor state snapshot, kernel handler entry, and restored state return.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book", "riscv-unprivileged"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Virtual Memory Mapping",
       "claim": "Page tables map virtual addresses to physical memory with permission bits.",
       "example": "A page table entry translates a virtual address to a physical frame while enforcing read/write/execute permissions.",
       "visualDirection": "Render a page table structure with virtual address bits, physical address bits, and permission flags.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Process Scheduling",
       "claim": "The scheduler selects runnable processes and switches processor context.",
       "example": "A round-robin scheduler picks the next process from the ready queue and saves the current context.",
       "visualDirection": "Draw a scheduler loop showing ready queue, context save/restore, and CPU allocation.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Concurrency and Locking",
       "claim": "Locks protect invariants when concurrent execution can access shared state.",
       "example": "A mutex lock prevents two processes from modifying a shared counter simultaneously.",
       "visualDirection": "Show a lock acquisition/release sequence with a shared variable and protected critical section.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "File System Organization",
       "claim": "The file system organizes persistent data through inodes, directories, and logged updates.",
       "example": "An inode stores metadata for a file while directory entries link names to inode references.",
       "visualDirection": "Display a file system tree with inode blocks, directory entries, and journal log entries.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     },
     {
       "title": "RISC-V Instruction Set Basics",
       "claim": "The RISC-V ISA defines programmer-visible instructions, registers, memory operations, control transfer, exceptions, and ordering behavior.",
       "example": "Integer register x0 always reads as zero, while other registers hold software-visible values.",
       "visualDirection": "List RISC-V register file with x0 marked as zero and other registers showing software values.",
       "sourceIds": ["riscv-unprivileged"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Memory Ordering and Dependencies",
       "claim": "Memory-ordering rules constrain how memory operations may be observed.",
       "example": "Store buffers ensure that writes are visible to other cores in a defined architectural order.",
       "visualDirection": "Show a memory operation sequence with ordering constraints and dependency arrows.",
       "sourceIds": ["riscv-unprivileged"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
       "claim": "Analysis distinguishes user mode, privileged kernel code, hardware translation, interrupt state, resource ownership, and synchronization invariants.",
       "example": "A security audit checks for proper privilege separation and lock invariants in a kernel module.",
       "visualDirection": "Provide a checklist template with checkboxes for each analysis category.",
       "sourceIds": ["mit-xv6-book"]
     }
   ]}
